combined_design:
One Opus synthesis call per ayah over a compact, calibrated supply, with a pericope-level chain map before it and a hierarchical surah layer after it. B's methodology decides; B's ensemble stays a test arm.

(1) S0 supply, script, free. D's knowledge-aware packet is the base:
- the surah text as prefix;
- every branch as an index line, with phrases for memory_score ≤1;
- construction tags;
- root-level uses and partner profiles.

Added to it:
- C1's guard statuses and the top-3 typed neighbour-activation lines per word;
- C2's loaded-role section D and parallels section E only (C2's section C is the bulk, and C1 covers the same job at about half the size);
- A's harvest sections A, C titles and D where Tier 1/2 exist.

Budget with the c01 formula: ≤60k tokens for ayat under 40 words, ≤90k for longer ones. Overflow goes to an appended lookup only if V1 logs show it is read. No tool reads in production.

(2) S1 chain map, Opus, one call per pericope (about 351, plus about 35 whole short surahs):
- input ≤100k: A's lean index at 3.5k per ayah plus window-anchored channel subchannels, never the whole review;
- no set-asides;
- cost $1.3–1.7 CLI or about $0.6–0.8 Batch, about $0.04 per ayah.

(3) S2, one Opus effort-high call per ayah:
- memory permitted and marked, no audit instructions;
- output budgeted at 40–60k (never capped): about $0.5–0.8 Batch, $1.2–2.0 CLI;
- ayat of 40 words or more (109) and 2:282-class ayat through the API from the start.

(4) S3 script checks: sources, the branch_kind/construction guard, memory verification against the early entries, and an unused index (A's S3, B's step 4 and D's V1–V4 merged). S4: at most one repair on hard flags.

(5) Surah layer, hierarchical: pericope commentaries, then the surah commentary, through the API for long surahs. About $0.07 per ayah in Batch.

(6) Tier 3: A's V3 (12 Luna calls, about $1) first. If it passes, S0b at $71–143 list-equivalent and about 1.5 days of wall time; otherwise Tier 3 runs on channels, v12 and the script layers, declared as thinner.

(7) Evaluation: B's frozen scorecard, 2 replicates, seeded blind ayat and the contamination lint. B's Step 1 integration pilot on existing readings ($8–9) is the only test of the ensemble; the two-reading integration enters production only for ayat classes where it beats F1 beyond noise.

(8) Infrastructure: build the Batch runner before scaling. Order the prefix as brief → surah text → pericope map → ayah packet, with a 1-hour TTL on the surah prefix and the first ayah of each surah submitted first. Measure a whole short blind surah (S88) end to end before any bulk run.

Estimated production: about $0.75–0.95 per ayah, about $4.7–5.9k for the Quran. Tests: about $1.3–2.0 per ayah for short and medium ayat on the CLI, and about $1.5–2.5 for long ayat on the API. Every call stays under $5.

Why this combination: it keeps the cheapest verified parts (D's packet, C1's guard and paths, A's harvest, the script checks). It removes the pieces that fail on cost or feasibility (C2's section C bulk, B's five-call primary as the default, single-call S5 for long surahs, tool reads in production). It moves the calls that cannot run on the CLI to the API, where the recorded ledgers show they must go.
